#!/bin/bash
# qubes-guix-create — run in dom0. Creates a Guix System qube and boots it
# from an installer ISO: the qubes-guix one (iso/qubes-guix-iso), which dom0
# drives over qrexec, or the stock Guix one.
#
#   qubes-guix-create [options] NAME ISO_VM:ISO_PATH [VCPUS [MEMORY [MAXMEM]]]
#   qubes-guix-create --unattended [options] NAME ISO_VM:ISO_PATH ...
#                                   # create and install, in one go
#   qubes-guix-create --appvm TEMPLATE [options] NAME [MEMORY [MAXMEM]]
#                                   # an AppVM on a Guix template
#
# Part of the qubes-guix channel: https://github.com/BetoHydroxyButyrate/qubes-guix

set -euo pipefail

prog=${0##*/}

# Defaults. MEMORY is what the qube gets for the whole install: there is no
# memory balancing until the Qubes agent runs, and `guix system init`
# needs more than a desktop does.
# MAXMEM defaults to MEMORY, i.e. no memory balancing. An HVM with
# MAXMEM > MEMORY boots in Xen populate-on-demand mode, which is slow:
# measured 24 s with 8000/8000 against 83 s with 8000/32542.
vcpus=2
memory=4000
maxmem=
label=purple
root_size=60g
netvm=
template=0
type_network=0
bootstrap=0
finish_install=0
install=0
force=0
no_password=0
locale=en_US.utf8
wait_start=1
packages_file=
channels_file=
subs_file=
guix_commit=
first_update=1
run_test=0
appvm_template=
split_ssh=0
split_gpg=0
ctap=0
run_now=0
branch=main
branch_given=0
qubes_iso=auto
repo_url=https://github.com/BetoHydroxyButyrate/qubes-guix
dry_run=0

usage() {
    cat <<EOF
Usage: $prog [options] NAME ISO_VM:ISO_PATH [VCPUS [MEMORY [MAXMEM]]]
       $prog --unattended [options] NAME ISO_VM:ISO_PATH [VCPUS [MEMORY [MAXMEM]]]
       $prog --type-network NAME
       $prog --appvm TEMPLATE [-l COLOUR] [-n NETVM] [--split-ssh]
                              [--split-gpg] [--ctap] [--test] NAME
                              [MEMORY [MAXMEM]]
       $prog --bootstrap [--template] [--branch B] [--packages FILE] NAME
       $prog --finish-install [--template] [--branch B] [--packages FILE]
                              [--run] NAME
       $prog --unattended [--template] [--branch B] [--no-password]
                          [--force] [--locale L] [--packages FILE]
                          [--guix-commit C] [--substitutes FILE]
                          [--no-update] [--test] [--no-wait] NAME

Create the StandaloneVM HVM qube NAME and boot it from the installer ISO
at ISO_PATH in the qube ISO_VM (or dom0:/path). With the qubes-guix
installer ISO (iso/qubes-guix-iso), the installer has its network and the
qrexec agent, and dom0 drives it with qvm-run: no GRUB edit, no typing into
its console. With the stock Guix ISO, the steps are printed. Given an ISO,
--unattended creates the qube and installs it in one go (qubes-guix ISO).

  VCPUS    virtual CPUs                         (default $vcpus)
  MEMORY   initial memory, MiB                  (default $memory)
  MAXMEM   memory-balancing maximum, MiB        (default: = MEMORY)
           MAXMEM > MEMORY turns memory balancing on, but slows every
           boot (Xen populate-on-demand); keep the gap small.
           (Qubes ties the meminfo-writer service to maxmem: setting the
           service resets maxmem to 0, so only maxmem is set here.)

An AppVM on a Guix template (--appvm) needs what Qubes doesn't give an
AppVM by default: HVM, its kernel from the template's disk (kernel ''), more
memory than 400 MiB, a longer qrexec timeout, and memory balancing (maxmem).
--appvm TEMPLATE creates NAME with all of that (MEMORY default 2000, MAXMEM
default MEMORY + 4000; MAXMEM = MEMORY: no balancing). With --test, then
runs qubes-guix-test on it.

Options:
  -l, --label COLOUR    window label            (default $label)
  -s, --size SIZE       root volume size        (default $root_size)
  -n, --netvm QUBE      network qube            (default: the system default)
  -t, --template        make a TemplateVM (AppVMs can be based on it)
                        instead of a StandaloneVM
      --split-ssh       tag the qube split-ssh (the README's qubes.SshAgent
                        policy allows that tag)
      --split-gpg       turn on its split-gpg2-client service
      --ctap            turn on its qubes-ctap-proxy and qubes-u2f-proxy
                        services (and allow it in Global Config, USB
                        Devices, U2F Proxy)
                        These three are for NAME itself: on a template, for
                        the template; each AppVM gets its own (--appvm ...
                        --split-ssh). With --test, the test checks
                        split-ssh and split-gpg2.
      --qubes-iso       ISO_PATH is the qubes-guix installer ISO: dom0 waits
                        for its qrexec agent (assumed with --unattended, or
                        when the file name contains 'qubes-guix')
      --stock-iso       ISO_PATH is the stock Guix installer ISO
      --dry-run         print the dom0 commands, change nothing; with the
                        typing modes below, print what would be typed
      --type-network    for a running NAME without its Qubes agent (the
                        installer, or the installed system before setup):
                        click its console window, and this switches it to
                        the root shell (Ctrl+Alt+F3) and types the network
                        setup there, with NAME's address, gateway and DNS
      --bootstrap       likewise, but types a script into /tmp on NAME and
                        shows it without running it: network, then clone
                        this repository and run its guest setup as the
                        uid-1000 user (with --template: for a template)
      --finish-install  at the installer's "Installation complete" screen,
                        BEFORE rebooting: clones this repository into /root
                        on the installer (VT3) and shows the command that
                        makes the new system a qube before its first boot
                        (guest/qubes-guix-finish-install). Needs the
                        installer's network (--type-network) still up.
      --run             with --finish-install: run that command too, rather
                        than leaving it for you to review and type
      --unattended      the whole install, unattended, instead of the
                        installer's dialogs. On the qubes-guix ISO, over
                        qrexec, its output shown here (with an ISO given,
                        right after creating NAME). On the stock ISO, run it
                        at the installer's first screen (after the GRUB
                        'nomodeset' step), and it types. Asks here
                        for a password for the user and root (stored as a
                        hash; --no-password: none), then types into VT3 the
                        network setup, a clone of this repository and
                        guest/qubes-guix-install: partition /dev/xvda,
                        write config.scm (Xfce; user 'user'; dom0's
                        timezone), qubes-guix-finish-install, power off.
                        Then waits for NAME to stop and starts it from its
                        disk (--no-wait: don't). --force: wipe a disk that
                        already has partitions. --locale: default en_US.utf8
                        A template is left without a netvm afterwards: it
                        updates through the Qubes updates proxy.
      --guix-commit C   with --unattended: lock the qube's Guix at commit C
                        (#:guix-commit in its config.scm; move it later with
                        qubes-guix-update --guix-commit C, or --head).
                        Default: 'iso' from the qubes-guix ISO, else 'server'
                        when there are substitute servers (--substitutes),
                        else follow the head; 'head' follows the head
                        either way. 'iso': Guix and qubes at the ISO's own
                        commits (what it was built from: e.g. your HEAD, with
                        iso/qubes-guix-iso-remote), the other channels as
                        'server'. 'server': every channel
                        at the commits the first --substitutes server last
                        built (its /channel-commits, recorded as
                        #:channel-commits; Guix alone from /guix-commit if
                        that's all it publishes), so the install and
                        updates match its builds.
      --test            with --unattended: after the first update, run
                        qubes-guix-test (next to this script) on NAME: a
                        throwaway AppVM for a template, else NAME itself.
                        Its failures make this exit non-zero.
      --no-update       with --unattended: skip the first update. Normally,
                        once installed (built with the installer's own Guix,
                        for speed), NAME is started and 'qubes-guix-update
                        -r' run in it: to the head, or to the --guix-commit
                        lock. A template is then shut down, ready.
      --channels FILE   with --unattended, --finish-install, --bootstrap:
                        extra channels, FILE holding (channel ...) forms
                        (default: qubes-guix-channels in the current
                        directory, else next to this script). They go into config.scm as
                        #:extra-channels; --unattended and --finish-install
                        also build with them, so --packages can name their
                        packages.
      --substitutes FILE
                        with --unattended, --finish-install: extra
                        substitute servers, e.g. your own 'guix publish' or
                        cache (default: qubes-guix-substitutes in the current
                        directory, else next to this script). FILE has lines
                          url http://host/...
                          key (public-key (ecc (curve Ed25519) (q #...#)))
                        They're tried before the default servers for the
                        install, and recorded in config.scm
                        (#:extra-substitute-urls, #:extra-authorized-keys)
                        for later updates. With --guix-commit, the install
                        also builds at that commit: lock to the commit your
                        server built, and nearly everything comes from it.
      --packages FILE   with --unattended, --finish-install, --bootstrap:
                        extra packages for config.scm (#:extra-packages),
                        one or more per line, # starts a comment (default:
                        qubes-guix-packages in the current directory, else
                        next to this script; else just git). Names Guix doesn't have are skipped
                        with a warning, not an error.
  -b, --branch B        the qubes-guix branch that --bootstrap,
                        --finish-install and --unattended clone and build
                        from (default main; on the qubes-guix ISO, its own
                        scripts and branch, without cloning)
  -h, --help            this help

Examples:
  $prog --unattended guix work:/home/user/qubes-guix-installer.iso
  $prog guix untrusted:/home/user/Downloads/guix-system-install-1.5.0.x86_64-linux.iso
  $prog guix untrusted:/home/user/guix.iso 4 4000 16000
EOF
}

die() { echo "$prog: $*" >&2; exit 1; }

# Show, then run (unless --dry-run). Commands that change dom0 state go
# through this; read-only checks don't.
run() {
    printf '+'; printf ' %q' "$@"; printf '\n'
    if [ "$dry_run" = 0 ]; then "$@"; fi
}

is_uint() { case $1 in ''|*[!0-9]*) return 1;; esac; }

# The vault clients and U2F for qube $1, as chosen (--split-ssh,
# --split-gpg, --ctap). Not the services Qubes derives (meminfo-writer).
clients_setup() {
    [ "$split_ssh" = 1 ] && run qvm-tags "$1" add split-ssh
    [ "$split_gpg" = 1 ] && run qvm-service "$1" split-gpg2-client on
    if [ "$ctap" = 1 ]; then
        run qvm-service "$1" qubes-ctap-proxy on
        run qvm-service "$1" qubes-u2f-proxy on
    fi
    return 0
}

# qubes-guix-test on qube $1, checking the vault clients chosen.
run_qubes_guix_test() {
    local args=()
    [ "$split_ssh" = 1 ] && args+=(--split-ssh)
    [ "$split_gpg" = 1 ] && args+=(--split-gpg)
    echo "Testing $1: qubes-guix-test ${args[*]}"
    "$(dirname "$0")/qubes-guix-test" "${args[@]}" "$1" ||
        { echo "$prog: qubes-guix-test found problems (above)" >&2; exit 1; }
}

# as_desktop CMD...: run CMD as dom0's desktop user. The Qubes menu
# (qvm-appmenus, qvm-sync-appmenus) lives in that user's home; as root it
# fails or writes root's menus, which nobody sees. The user: sudo's caller,
# else whoever runs qubes-guid (the GUI daemon drawing the qubes' windows).
as_desktop() {
    local u
    if [ "$(id -u)" != 0 ]; then "$@"; return; fi
    u=${SUDO_USER:-}
    [ -n "$u" ] && [ "$u" != root ] ||
        u=$(ps -o user= -C qubes-guid 2>/dev/null | grep -vx root | head -n1)
    [ -n "$u" ] || u=$(getent group qubes | cut -d: -f4 | cut -d, -f1)
    [ -n "$u" ] || { echo "$prog: no desktop user found for: $*" >&2; return 1; }
    runuser -u "$u" -- "$@"
}

# --- arguments ---------------------------------------------------------

args=()
while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    case $1 in
        -l|--label) [ $# -ge 2 ] || die "$1 needs a value"; label=$2; shift 2;;
        -s|--size)  [ $# -ge 2 ] || die "$1 needs a value"; root_size=$2; shift 2;;
        -n|--netvm) [ $# -ge 2 ] || die "$1 needs a value"; netvm=$2; shift 2;;
        -t|--template) template=1; shift;;
        --type-network) type_network=1; shift;;
        --bootstrap) bootstrap=1; shift;;
        --finish-install) finish_install=1; shift;;
        --run)      run_now=1; shift;;
        --unattended|--install) install=1; shift;;
        --force)    force=1; shift;;
        --no-password) no_password=1; shift;;
        --locale)   [ $# -ge 2 ] || die "$1 needs a value"; locale=$2; shift 2;;
        --no-wait)  wait_start=0; shift;;
        --packages) [ $# -ge 2 ] || die "$1 needs a value"; packages_file=$2; shift 2;;
        --channels) [ $# -ge 2 ] || die "$1 needs a value"; channels_file=$2; shift 2;;
        --substitutes) [ $# -ge 2 ] || die "$1 needs a value"; subs_file=$2; shift 2;;
        --guix-commit) [ $# -ge 2 ] || die "$1 needs a value"; guix_commit=$2; shift 2;;
        --no-update) first_update=0; shift;;
        --test)     run_test=1; shift;;
        --appvm)    [ $# -ge 2 ] || die "$1 needs a TEMPLATE"; appvm_template=$2; shift 2;;
        --split-ssh) split_ssh=1; shift;;
        --split-gpg) split_gpg=1; shift;;
        --ctap)     ctap=1; shift;;
        -b|--branch) [ $# -ge 2 ] || die "$1 needs a value"; branch=$2; branch_given=1; shift 2;;
        --qubes-iso) qubes_iso=1; shift;;
        --stock-iso) qubes_iso=0; shift;;
        --dry-run)  dry_run=1; shift;;
        -h|--help)  usage; exit 0;;
        --)         shift; args+=("$@"); break;;
        -*)         die "unknown option $1 (see --help)";;
        *)          args+=("$1"); shift;;
    esac
done

# --- typing into a qube's console (--type-network, --bootstrap,
#     --finish-install) --------------------------------------------------

# The network settings dom0 has for qube $1: sets ip, gw, dns.
qube_net() {
    ip=$(qvm-prefs "$1" ip 2>/dev/null) || ip=
    [ -n "$ip" ] || die "$1 has no IP address (no netvm?)"
    gw=$(qvm-prefs "$1" visible_gateway)
    dns=$(qvm-prefs "$1" dns | xargs)
    [ -n "$dns" ] || dns="10.139.1.1 10.139.1.2"
}

# Type TEXT into the root shell on VT3 of qube NAME's console window.
# The VT switch is a synthetic event (--window): a real Ctrl+Alt+F3 would be
# caught by dom0's own X server and switch dom0's console. The text is real
# (XTEST) key presses to the focused window: synthetic ones lose Shift in the
# Qubes GUI daemon ($ arrives as 4, | as \). Keep TEXT free of tabs (they'd
# trigger completion) and of '!' (bash history expansion).
type_into() {
    local name=$1 text=$2 win title
    if [ "$dry_run" = 1 ]; then
        echo "Dry run: would type into $name's console, after Ctrl+Alt+F3 and Enter:"
        printf '%s\n' "$text" | sed 's/^/  | /'
        return 0
    fi
    command -v xdotool >/dev/null 2>&1 || die "xdotool not found in dom0"
    qvm-check -q --running "$name" 2>/dev/null || die "$name isn't running"
    echo "Click $name's console window (the cursor is a cross); then hands off"
    echo "keyboard and mouse until the typing stops."
    win=$(xdotool selectwindow) || die "no window selected"
    # Only into NAME's window: what's typed is a root shell's commands, and
    # the wrong click (a dom0 terminal, the desktop) would send them there.
    title=$(xdotool getwindowname "$win" 2>/dev/null) || title=
    case $title in
        *"$name"*) ;;
        *) die "the window clicked ('${title:-no title}') isn't $name's console: nothing typed";;
    esac
    xdotool key --window "$win" ctrl+alt+F3
    sleep 1
    xdotool windowactivate --sync "$win"
    # The installer's VT3 first says "[press ENTER to login]"; in a shell
    # that's already open, an empty line does nothing.
    xdotool key Return
    sleep 1
    # One line at a time, each ended by a Return key press: a newline inside
    # 'xdotool type' doesn't reach the console as Enter.
    local line
    while IFS= read -r line; do
        [ -n "$line" ] && xdotool type --delay 12 -- "$line"
        xdotool key Return
        sleep 0.2
    done <<< "$text"
}

# A TemplateVM gets the template setup whether or not --template was given:
# forgetting it gives a template without /rw, /home or the updates proxy.
# For an existing qube: --template follows its class. A TemplateVM is set
# up as a template even without --template; --template on any other class
# is refused: the template setup (no netvm afterwards, updates through
# qubes.UpdatesProxy, which dom0's policy allows for @type:TemplateVM)
# would leave a StandaloneVM or AppVM unable to update.
detect_template() {
    local klass
    klass=$(qvm-prefs "$1" klass 2>/dev/null) || die "no qube named '$1'"
    if [ "$klass" = TemplateVM ]; then
        if [ "$template" = 0 ]; then
            echo "$1 is a TemplateVM: setting it up as a template (--template)."
            template=1
        fi
    elif [ "$template" = 1 ]; then
        die "$1 is a $klass, not a TemplateVM: --template needs one. Leave out
--template to set it up as a $klass, or make a TemplateVM (qubes-guix-create
--template NAME) and install there."
    fi
}

# Extra packages, into $packages: from --packages FILE, or qubes-guix-packages
# next to this script. Checked here: they're typed into a root shell.
# Extra channels, into $channels_text: from --channels FILE, or
# qubes-guix-channels next to this script. (channel ...) forms, typed into
# the qube as a file, so: tabs become spaces (a tab would trigger completion),
# and no line may be the here-document's end marker.
# The data file NAME (qubes-guix-packages, -channels, -substitutes) when no
# option names one: in the current directory, else next to this script;
# nothing if neither has it.
data_file() {
    if [ -f "./$1" ]; then
        echo "$PWD/$1"
    elif [ -f "$(dirname "$0")/$1" ]; then
        echo "$(dirname "$0")/$1"
    fi
}

read_channels() {
    if [ -z "$channels_file" ]; then
        channels_file=$(data_file qubes-guix-channels)
    fi
    channels_text=
    if [ -n "$channels_file" ]; then
        [ -r "$channels_file" ] || die "can't read $channels_file"
        channels_text=$(expand "$channels_file")
        if ! printf '%s\n' "$channels_text" | sed 's/;.*//' | grep -q '(channel'; then
            echo "Extra channels: none in $channels_file (all commented out?)"
            channels_text=
            return 0
        fi
        printf '%s\n' "$channels_text" | grep -qx 'QGCH' &&
            die "$channels_file: a line 'QGCH' would end the typed file early"
        echo "Extra channels (from $channels_file):"
        printf '%s\n' "$channels_text" | sed 's/;.*//' |
            sed -n "s/.*(name '\\([^) ]*\\).*/  \\1/p; s/.*(name (quote \\([^) ]*\\)).*/  \\1/p" || true
    fi
}

# Shell lines that write $channels_text to file $1 in the qube.
channels_heredoc() {
    printf "cat > %s <<'QGCH'\n%s\nQGCH\n" "$1" "$channels_text"
}

# Extra substitute servers, into $subs_text: from --substitutes FILE, or
# qubes-guix-substitutes next to this script. Only "url URL" and
# "key (public-key ...)" lines (comments and blank lines dropped), checked
# here: they're typed into a root shell as a file.
read_substitutes() {
    if [ -z "$subs_file" ]; then
        subs_file=$(data_file qubes-guix-substitutes)
    fi
    subs_text=
    if [ -z "$subs_file" ]; then
        echo "Substitute servers: no qubes-guix-substitutes here or next to $prog"
        echo "(a qubes-guix ISO built with one brings its own)."
        return 0
    fi
    [ -r "$subs_file" ] || die "can't read $subs_file"
    # Comments are whole lines only: keys contain '#'.
    subs_text=$(sed 's/^[[:space:]]*#.*//; s/^[[:space:]]*//; s/[[:space:]]*$//; /^$/d' "$subs_file" | expand)
    if [ -z "$subs_text" ]; then
        echo "Substitute servers: none in $subs_file (all commented out?)"
        return 0
    fi
    local line
    while IFS= read -r line; do
        case $line in
            "url "*)
                case ${line#url } in
                    http://*|https://*) ;;
                    *) die "$subs_file: not an http(s) URL: $line";;
                esac
                case ${line#url } in
                    *[!A-Za-z0-9:/._~%-]*) die "$subs_file: odd characters in: $line";;
                esac;;
            "key (public-key "*")")
                case $line in
                    *"'"*|*'$'*|*'`'*|*'\'*|*'!'*) die "$subs_file: odd characters in: $line";;
                esac;;
            *) die "$subs_file: expected 'url URL' or 'key (public-key ...)' on one line, found: $line";;
        esac
    done <<< "$subs_text"
    printf '%s\n' "$subs_text" | grep -q '^url ' || die "$subs_file: no 'url ...' line"
    echo "Substitute servers (from $subs_file):"
    printf '%s\n' "$subs_text" | sed -n 's/^url /  /p'
}

# Shell lines that write $subs_text to file $1 in the qube.
subs_heredoc() {
    printf "cat > %s <<'QGSU'\n%s\nQGSU\n" "$1" "$subs_text"
}

read_packages() {
    if [ -z "$packages_file" ]; then
        packages_file=$(data_file qubes-guix-packages)
    fi
    packages=
    if [ -n "$packages_file" ]; then
        [ -r "$packages_file" ] || die "can't read $packages_file"
        packages=$(sed 's/#.*//' "$packages_file" | xargs)
        for p in $packages; do
            case $p in
                *[!A-Za-z0-9@._+-]*|[!A-Za-z0-9]*) die "bad package name '$p' in $packages_file";;
            esac
        done
        echo "Extra packages (from $packages_file): ${packages:-none}"
    fi
}

typed=Typed
[ "$dry_run" = 1 ] && typed="Dry run, not typed"

# Root-shell commands that bring up the first non-loopback interface with
# the address, gateway and DNS from qube_net: eth0 in the installer, maybe
# not after it. NetworkManager (installed system) would fight a static setup.
net_cmds() {
    printf '%s' "herd stop NetworkManager 2>/dev/null; \
IF=\$(ls /sys/class/net | grep -v '^lo\$' | head -n1); \
ip addr replace $ip/8 dev \$IF; ip link set \$IF up; \
ip route replace default via $gw; \
printf 'nameserver %s\\n' $dns > /etc/resolv.conf; \
ip -br addr show \$IF; ip route"
}

# --- the qubes-guix installer ISO: driven over qrexec --------------------

# Whether qube $1 answers qrexec as root. The qubes-guix installer ISO
# (iso/qubes-guix-iso) runs the agent, the stock Guix installer doesn't.
has_qrexec() {
    [ "$(qvm-features "$1" qrexec 2>/dev/null)" = 1 ] || return 1
    timeout 30 qvm-run -q -u root --no-gui --pass-io "$1" true </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1
}

# The qubes channel branch for a job in the installer: -b if given, else the
# one the ISO's guix has (so its prebuilt Qubes packages are used), else main.
# Shell lines, run in the qube, that set B.
guest_branch_lines() {
    if [ "$branch_given" = 1 ]; then
        printf "B='%s'\n" "$branch"
    else
        # shellcheck disable=SC2016  # expanded in the qube
        printf '%s\n' \
            'B=$(guix describe 2>/dev/null | awk '"'"'NF == 2 && $1 !~ /:$/ { n = $1 } $1 == "branch:" && n == "qubes" { print $2; exit }'"'"')' \
            '[ -n "$B" ] || B=main'
    fi
}

# Shell lines, run in the qube first, that get the guest scripts: the ISO's
# copy in /root/qubes-guix, or with -b a fresh clone of that branch.
guest_repo_lines() {
    local d=/root/qubes-guix
    if [ "$branch_given" = 1 ]; then
        printf '%s\n' "rm -rf $d; git clone -q -b $branch $repo_url $d"
    fi
    printf '%s\n' "[ -f $d/guest/qubes-guix-install ] || { echo \"$d/guest is missing: is $name running the qubes-guix installer ISO?\" >&2; exit 1; }"
}

# Run CMD (shell lines) as root in qube NAME over qrexec, detached from this
# terminal (an install outlives a closed dom0 window), and follow its log
# here. PRELUDE runs first, in the foreground: it writes the files CMD
# needs. With POWEROFF=1 the qube powers off once CMD succeeds.
# Sets job_status: CMD's exit status, or "off" if the qube went off first.
qrexec_job() {
    local name=$1 job=$2 prelude=$3 cmd=$4 poweroff=$5
    local log=/root/$job.log st=/root/$job.status runner=/root/$job.sh
    local off=':'
    [ "$poweroff" = 1 ] &&
        off='sleep 5; herd stop cow-store /mnt >/dev/null 2>&1; umount /mnt 2>/dev/null; halt'
    local script="set -eu
$prelude
rm -f $st
cat > $runner <<'QGRUN'
{
$cmd
}
s=\$?
echo \$s > $st
[ \$s = 0 ] && { $off; }
QGRUN
setsid sh $runner > $log 2>&1 < /dev/null &
echo \$! > /root/$job.pid"
    job_status=
    if [ "$dry_run" = 1 ]; then
        echo "Dry run: would run as root in $name, over qrexec:"
        printf '%s\n' "$script" | sed 's/^/  | /'
        job_status=0
        return 0
    fi
    printf '%s\n' "$script" | qvm-run -q -u root --pass-io --no-gui "$name" 'sh -s' ||
        die "couldn't start $job in $name"
    echo "Following its log ($log in $name). Ctrl+C stops this script, not the"
    echo "$job; to follow it again:  qvm-run -u root --pass-io $name 'tail -f $log'"
    qvm-run -q -u root --pass-io --no-gui "$name" \
        "tail -n +1 --pid=\$(cat /root/$job.pid) -F $log 2>/dev/null" </dev/null || true
    # The log ended: the job is done, or the qube went off with it.
    while qvm-check -q --running "$name" 2>/dev/null; do
        job_status=$(timeout 30 qvm-run -q -u root --pass-io --no-gui "$name" \
                         "cat $st 2>/dev/null" </dev/null 2>/dev/null) || job_status=
        if [ -n "$job_status" ] && { [ "$job_status" != 0 ] || [ "$poweroff" = 0 ]; }; then
            return 0
        fi
        sleep 10
    done
    job_status=${job_status:-off}
}

# --- an AppVM on a Guix template (--appvm) -----------------------------

if [ -n "$appvm_template" ]; then
    [ ${#args[@]} -ge 1 ] && [ ${#args[@]} -le 3 ] || { usage >&2; exit 2; }
    name=${args[0]}
    memory=${args[1]:-2000}
    is_uint "$memory" || die "MEMORY must be an integer (MiB)"
    maxmem=${args[2]:-$((memory + 4000))}
    is_uint "$maxmem" || die "MAXMEM must be an integer (MiB)"
    [ "$maxmem" -ge "$memory" ] || die "MAXMEM ($maxmem) is less than MEMORY ($memory)"
    [[ $name =~ ^[a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9_.-]{0,30}$ ]] ||
        die "invalid qube name '$name' (letter first; letters, digits, _ . -; max 31)"
    qvm-check -q "$name" 2>/dev/null && die "a qube named '$name' already exists"
    [ "$(qvm-prefs "$appvm_template" klass 2>/dev/null)" = TemplateVM ] ||
        die "'$appvm_template' isn't a TemplateVM"
    [ "$(qvm-features "$appvm_template" os-distribution 2>/dev/null)" = guix ] ||
        echo "$prog: warning: $appvm_template doesn't report os-distribution guix (not started since its install?)" >&2
    if [ -n "$netvm" ]; then
        qvm-check -q "$netvm" 2>/dev/null || die "network qube '$netvm' doesn't exist"
    fi
    echo "Creating $name: AppVM on $appvm_template, HVM, $memory-$maxmem MiB"
    run qvm-create --template "$appvm_template" --label "$label" \
        --property virt_mode=hvm "$name"
    # Memory balancing is maxmem alone (0: off). Not the meminfo-writer
    # service: Qubes derives it from maxmem, and setting it resets maxmem
    # to 0.
    [ "$maxmem" -gt "$memory" ] || maxmem=0
    run qvm-prefs "$name" kernel ''
    run qvm-prefs "$name" memory "$memory"
    run qvm-prefs "$name" maxmem "$maxmem"
    run qvm-prefs "$name" qrexec_timeout 300
    [ -n "$netvm" ] && run qvm-prefs "$name" netvm "$netvm"
    clients_setup "$name"
    # Its Qubes menu: the template's default-menu-items (what the Guix agent
    # asked for: the usual applications that the template has), as Qubes
    # does for a new AppVM when it can; set here so it doesn't depend on
    # dom0 having read them in time.
    items=$(qvm-features "$appvm_template" default-menu-items 2>/dev/null) || items=
    if [ -n "$items" ]; then
        menu=$(mktemp)
        printf '%s\n' $items > "$menu"
        chmod 644 "$menu"
        echo "Menu: $items"
        run as_desktop qvm-appmenus --set-whitelist "$menu" "$name" ||
            echo "$prog: note: couldn't set $name's menu: add its items in its Settings (Applications)" >&2
        run as_desktop qvm-appmenus --update "$name" || true
        rm -f "$menu"
    else
        echo "$prog: note: $appvm_template has no default-menu-items: add $name's menu
items in its Settings (Applications)" >&2
    fi
    if [ "$run_test" = 1 ] && [ "$dry_run" = 0 ]; then
        run_qubes_guix_test "$name"
    else
        echo "Created. Start it with:  qvm-start $name"
    fi
    exit 0
fi

if [ "$type_network" = 1 ]; then
    [ ${#args[@]} -eq 1 ] || { usage >&2; exit 2; }
    name=${args[0]}
    if [ "$dry_run" = 0 ] && has_qrexec "$name"; then
        echo "$name answers qrexec (the qubes-guix ISO): its network comes from QubesDB."
        qvm-run -q -u root --pass-io --no-gui "$name" 'ip -br addr; ip route' </dev/null || true
        exit 0
    fi
    qube_net "$name"
    # The first non-loopback interface: eth0 in the installer, maybe not
    # after it. NetworkManager (installed system) would fight a static setup.
    type_into "$name" "$(net_cmds)
"
    cat <<EOF
$typed into $name's console (VT3): $ip/8 via $gw, DNS $dns
The last lines on its screen show the result. Back to the installer:
  xdotool key --window \$(xdotool selectwindow) ctrl+alt+F1
EOF
    exit 0
fi

if [ "$bootstrap" = 1 ]; then
    [ ${#args[@]} -eq 1 ] || { usage >&2; exit 2; }
    name=${args[0]}
    detect_template "$name"
    qube_net "$name"
    setup_args=
    [ "$template" = 1 ] && setup_args=--template
    read_packages
    read_channels
    chf=/tmp/qubes-guix-channels.scm
    ch_lines=
    [ -n "$channels_text" ] && ch_lines="$(channels_heredoc $chf)"
    f=/tmp/qubes-guix-bootstrap.sh
    # Written to a file on the qube and shown, NOT run: read it, then run it.
    # As root: the network (Qubes has no DHCP); then as the first regular
    # user: clone this repository and run guest/qubes-guix-setup.
    type_into "$name" "set +H; cat > $f <<'QGEOF'
# qubes-guix bootstrap, typed in by qubes-guix-create (dom0). Review, then:
#   sh $f
set -eu
IP=$ip GW=$gw DNS='$dns' BRANCH=$branch SETUP_ARGS='$setup_args'
PKGS='$packages'
CHANNELS=$( [ -n "$channels_text" ] && echo "$chf")
$ch_lines
herd stop NetworkManager 2>/dev/null || true
IF=\$(ls /sys/class/net | grep -v '^lo\$' | head -n1)
ip addr replace \$IP/8 dev \$IF
ip link set \$IF up
ip route replace default via \$GW
printf 'nameserver %s\\n' \$DNS > /etc/resolv.conf
U=\$(id -un 1000 2>/dev/null)
[ -n \"\$U\" ] || { echo 'no user with uid 1000'; exit 1; }
echo \"network up on \$IF; running the setup as \$U\"
# su -c gives no controlling terminal, and sudo (in the setup) needs one
# for its password prompt: 'script' supplies a pty.
su - \"\$U\" -c \"script -qec 'set -e; cd; if [ -d qubes-guix ]; then guix shell git -- git -C qubes-guix fetch -q origin \$BRANCH; guix shell git -- git -C qubes-guix checkout -q -B \$BRANCH FETCH_HEAD; else guix shell git -- git clone -b \$BRANCH $repo_url; fi; QUBES_GUIX_BRANCH=\$BRANCH QUBES_PACKAGES=\\\"\$PKGS\\\" QUBES_CHANNELS_FILE=\$CHANNELS qubes-guix/guest/qubes-guix-setup \$SETUP_ARGS' /dev/null\"
QGEOF
printf '\\033c'; cat $f; echo; echo 'Review the above, then run:  sh $f'
"
    cat <<EOF
$typed into $name's console (VT3): $f, shown there but not run.
Read it on the console, then run:  sh $f
(It sets the network, then runs the setup as the uid-1000 user, branch
$branch$( [ "$template" = 1 ] && echo ', as a template'). sudo asks that user's password once.)
EOF
    exit 0
fi

# --- --unattended -------------------------------------------------------

# After an unattended install has powered NAME off: a template loses its
# netvm; NAME starts from its disk; the first update; the test.
start_installed() {
    if [ "$template" = 1 ]; then
        # Like other templates: no network; updates (guix pull,
        # substitutes) go through the Qubes updates proxy.
        echo "$name is off. As a template it now gets no netvm (updates go through"
        echo "the updates proxy). To give it one back:  qvm-prefs $name netvm <qube>"
        qvm-prefs "$name" netvm ''
    fi
    echo "Starting $name."
    echo "Installed in $(elapsed)."
    # dom0 only starts a qube's qrexec daemon, and qvm-start only waits
    # for it, if the qube has the qrexec feature. A new qube doesn't: the
    # agent asks for it on its first boot, too late for this start. The
    # installed system has the agent, so set it now (setting it before
    # the install would make qvm-start wait for, and kill, a stock
    # installer; the qubes-guix ISO has it set already).
    qvm-features "$name" qrexec 1
    qvm-start "$name"
    if [ "$first_update" = 1 ]; then
        # The install built with the installer's Guix (fast: substitutes).
        # Now bring the system to the Guix it follows: the head, or the
        # --guix-commit lock recorded in its config.scm.
        echo "Updating $name: qubes-guix-update -r (pull to $( [ -n "$guix_commit" ] && echo "the locked commit" || echo 'the head'), then reconfigure)."
        echo "This is long: it builds the Qubes packages against that Guix. Output follows."
        if qvm-run -q -u root --pass-io --no-gui "$name" 'qubes-guix-update -r' </dev/null; then
            echo "Updated in $(elapsed) in all."
            # The Qubes menu: dom0 reads the updated system's applications.
            # The update sends them too (qubes.SyncAppMenus); this is for when
            # dom0's policy refused that.
            as_desktop qvm-sync-appmenus "$name" >/dev/null 2>&1 ||
                echo "$prog: note: qvm-sync-appmenus $name failed; refresh its applications in its Settings" >&2
            if [ "$template" = 1 ]; then
                qvm-shutdown --wait "$name"
                echo "$name is ready: shut down, updated. Base AppVMs on it."
            fi
            if [ "$run_test" = 1 ]; then
                run_qubes_guix_test "$name"
            fi
        else
            echo "$prog: the update in $name failed; it's running, at the installer's Guix." >&2
            echo "Look at the output above. To rerun it, from dom0:" >&2
            echo "  qvm-run -u root --pass-io --no-gui $name 'qubes-guix-update -r' </dev/null" >&2
            exit 1
        fi
    else
        echo "Started. Check from dom0:  qvm-run -u root --pass-io $name 'herd status qrexec-agent'"
    fi
}

# The password for 'user' and root, into $hash (crypt(3), from openssl): asked
# before anything slow happens, so nobody has to wait at the terminal.
ask_password() {
    hash_asked=1
    hash=
    if [ "$no_password" = 0 ] && [ "$dry_run" = 1 ]; then
        hash='<hash of the password you would be asked for>'
    elif [ "$no_password" = 0 ]; then
        command -v openssl >/dev/null 2>&1 ||
            die "openssl not found in dom0 (needed to hash the password); or use --no-password"
        [ -t 0 ] || die "no terminal to ask for the password on; use --no-password"
        # A qvm-run with this terminal as its stdin (an earlier run of this
        # script, say) can leave it non-blocking, and read then fails with
        # "Resource temporarily unavailable".
        python3 -c 'import fcntl, os; f = fcntl.fcntl(0, fcntl.F_GETFL); fcntl.fcntl(0, fcntl.F_SETFL, f & ~os.O_NONBLOCK)' 2>/dev/null || true
        while :; do
            read -rsp "Password for 'user' and root in $name: " pw1; echo
            read -rsp "Again: " pw2; echo
            [ -n "$pw1" ] && [ "$pw1" = "$pw2" ] && break
            echo "Empty, or they don't match; again."
        done
        hash=$(printf '%s\n' "$pw1" | openssl passwd -6 -stdin)
        unset pw1 pw2
    fi
}

elapsed() { local s=$(( $(date +%s) - t0 )); printf '%dh%02dm' $((s / 3600)) $((s % 3600 / 60)); }

# The unattended install of the running installer in qube $name: over
# qrexec on the qubes-guix ISO, else typed into the stock installer's VT3.
unattended() {
    detect_template "$name"
    local via_qrexec=0
    if [ "$dry_run" = 1 ]; then
        [ "$qubes_iso" = 1 ] && via_qrexec=1
    elif has_qrexec "$name"; then
        via_qrexec=1
    fi
    [ "$via_qrexec" = 1 ] || qube_net "$name"
    tz=$(timedatectl show -p Timezone --value 2>/dev/null) || tz=
    [ -n "$tz" ] || tz=$(readlink /etc/localtime 2>/dev/null | sed -n 's|.*/zoneinfo/||p')
    [ -n "$tz" ] || tz=UTC
    [ -n "${hash_asked:-}" ] || ask_password
    read_packages
    # Not /tmp: the installer's cow-store bind-mounts /mnt/tmp over it.
    d=/root/qubes-guix
    iargs="--host-name $(echo "$name" | tr '_' '-') --timezone $tz --locale $locale"
    [ "$template" = 1 ] && iargs="$iargs --template"
    [ "$force" = 1 ] && iargs="$iargs --force"
    [ -n "$hash" ] && iargs="$iargs --password-hash '$hash'"
    [ -n "$packages" ] && iargs="$iargs --packages '$packages'"
    t0=${t0:-$(date +%s)}
    read_channels
    ch_lines=
    if [ -n "$channels_text" ]; then
        ch_lines="$(channels_heredoc /root/qubes-guix-channels.scm)"
        iargs="$iargs --channels /root/qubes-guix-channels.scm"
    fi
    read_substitutes
    su_lines=
    if [ -n "$subs_text" ]; then
        su_lines="$(subs_heredoc /root/qubes-guix-substitutes)"
        iargs="$iargs --substitutes /root/qubes-guix-substitutes"
        # With your own server, lock to what it last built unless told
        # otherwise: its builds are then what the qube runs, and nothing is
        # built locally (the first update included). From the qubes-guix
        # ISO: to the ISO's guix and qubes (which may be newer than the
        # server's build: iso/qubes-guix-iso-remote), the rest the server's.
        if [ -z "$guix_commit" ] && [ "$via_qrexec" = 1 ]; then
            guix_commit=iso
            echo "Locking $name to the ISO's guix and qubes commits, and the other"
            echo "channels to your server's last build (--guix-commit server: all"
            echo "the server's; head: follow the head)."
        elif [ -z "$guix_commit" ]; then
            guix_commit=server
            echo "Locking $name to the commits your substitute server last built"
            echo "(--guix-commit head: follow the head instead)."
        fi
    fi
    case $guix_commit in
        ''|head) guix_commit=;;
        server) ;;
        iso) [ "$via_qrexec" = 1 ] ||
                 die "--guix-commit iso: only from the qubes-guix ISO (this installer isn't)";;
        *[!0-9a-f]*) die "--guix-commit: '$guix_commit' isn't a commit hash ('server', 'iso' or 'head')";;
    esac
    [ -n "$guix_commit" ] && iargs="$iargs --guix-commit $guix_commit"
    what="($( [ "$template" = 1 ] && echo 'template, ')timezone $tz, user 'user'$( [ -n "$hash" ] || echo ', no password'))"

    if [ "$via_qrexec" = 1 ]; then
        echo "$name runs the qubes-guix installer: installing over qrexec $what."
        echo "It partitions /dev/xvda, writes config.scm and runs guix system init"
        echo "with the ISO's Guix (its Qubes packages are prebuilt), then powers off."
        qrexec_job "$name" qubes-guix-install \
            "$(guest_repo_lines)
$ch_lines
$su_lines" \
            "$(guest_branch_lines)
sh $d/guest/qubes-guix-install $iargs -b \"\$B\"" 1
        if [ "$dry_run" = 1 ]; then
            echo "(dry run: would then start $name from its disk$( [ "$first_update" = 1 ] && echo ", run qubes-guix-update -r in it"))"
            return 0
        fi
        case $job_status in
            0|off) ;;
            *) die "the install in $name failed (exit $job_status). $name is still running
the installer: see /root/qubes-guix-install.log there, e.g.
  qvm-run -u root --pass-io $name 'tail -n 50 /root/qubes-guix-install.log'
Fix the cause and rerun:  $prog --unattended $name   (it resumes)";;
        esac
        if [ "$wait_start" = 1 ]; then
            start_installed
        else
            echo "$name is installed and off. Start it:  qvm-start $name"
        fi
        return 0
    fi

    cmd="sh $d/guest/qubes-guix-install $iargs -b $branch --poweroff"
    type_into "$name" "set +H
$(net_cmds)
$ch_lines
$su_lines
rm -rf $d; guix shell git -- git clone -q -b $branch $repo_url $d && $cmd"
    cat <<EOF
$typed into $name's console (VT3): the network setup, then a clone of branch
$branch and guest/qubes-guix-install $what.
It partitions /dev/xvda, builds a Guix with the qubes channel and the system
(an hour or more), then powers $name off. Watch VT3 for errors.
EOF
    if [ "$dry_run" = 1 ]; then
        echo "(dry run: would then wait for $name to power off$( [ "$template" = 1 ] && echo ", remove its netvm"), qvm-start $name$( [ "$first_update" = 1 ] && echo ", run qubes-guix-update -r in it$( [ "$template" = 1 ] && echo ' and shut it down')"))"
    elif [ "$wait_start" = 1 ]; then
        echo "Waiting for $name to power off, to start it from its disk (Ctrl+C: stop"
        echo "waiting; then start it yourself with: qvm-start $name)."
        while qvm-check -q --running "$name" 2>/dev/null; do sleep 15; done
        start_installed
    else
        echo "When it's off:  qvm-start $name"
    fi
}

if [ "$install" = 1 ] && [ ${#args[@]} -eq 1 ]; then
    name=${args[0]}
    unattended
    exit 0
fi

if [ "$finish_install" = 1 ]; then
    [ ${#args[@]} -eq 1 ] || { usage >&2; exit 2; }
    name=${args[0]}
    detect_template "$name"
    # Not /tmp: the installer's cow-store bind-mounts /mnt/tmp over it.
    d=/root/qubes-guix
    fargs=
    [ "$template" = 1 ] && fargs="--template"
    read_packages
    [ -n "$packages" ] && fargs="$fargs --packages '$packages'"
    read_channels
    ch_lines=
    if [ -n "$channels_text" ]; then
        ch_lines="$(channels_heredoc /root/qubes-guix-channels.scm)"
        fargs="$fargs --channels /root/qubes-guix-channels.scm"
    fi
    read_substitutes
    su_lines=
    if [ -n "$subs_text" ]; then
        su_lines="$(subs_heredoc /root/qubes-guix-substitutes)"
        fargs="$fargs --substitutes /root/qubes-guix-substitutes"
    fi
    # The qubes-guix ISO: run it over qrexec (--run is implied: nothing is
    # typed into a root shell), then restart NAME from its disk. dom0 does
    # the restart: a reboot inside the qube would boot the ISO again.
    if { [ "$dry_run" = 1 ] && [ "$qubes_iso" = 1 ]; } ||
       { [ "$dry_run" = 0 ] && has_qrexec "$name"; }; then
        echo "$name answers qrexec (the qubes-guix ISO): making the installed system a qube."
        qrexec_job "$name" qubes-guix-finish-install \
            "$(guest_repo_lines)
$ch_lines
$su_lines" \
            "$(guest_branch_lines)
sh $d/guest/qubes-guix-finish-install $fargs -b \"\$B\" && sync" 0
        [ "$dry_run" = 1 ] && exit 0
        [ "$job_status" = 0 ] ||
            die "finish-install in $name failed (exit $job_status): see
/root/qubes-guix-finish-install.log there; /mnt/etc/config.scm is unchanged."
        echo "Done. Restarting $name from its disk."
        qvm-shutdown --wait --timeout 180 "$name"
        qvm-start "$name"
        echo "$name is up: check with  qvm-run -u root --pass-io $name 'herd status'"
        exit 0
    fi
    cmd="sh $d/guest/qubes-guix-finish-install${fargs:+ $fargs} -b $branch"
    # The installer's shell is root, and its network was set up by
    # --type-network. git isn't in the installer image: guix shell fetches it.
    if [ "$run_now" = 1 ]; then
        next="$cmd"
    else
        next="{ printf '\\033c'; echo 'Cloned into $d. Review it, then run:'; echo '  $cmd'; }"
    fi
    type_into "$name" "set +H
$ch_lines
$su_lines
rm -rf $d; guix shell git -- git clone -q -b $branch $repo_url $d && $next"
    if [ "$run_now" = 1 ]; then
        cat <<EOF
$typed into $name's console (VT3): clone $branch into $d and run
  $cmd
It wraps /mnt/etc/config.scm and rebuilds /mnt with the qubes channel: the
first time it builds a Guix, which takes a while. Watch VT3 for 'Done', then
reboot (Ctrl+Alt+F1 back to the installer, or 'reboot' on VT3).
EOF
    else
        cat <<EOF
$typed into $name's console (VT3): clone branch $branch into $d.
Review it there, then run:
  $cmd
When it says Done, reboot.
EOF
    fi
    exit 0
fi

[ ${#args[@]} -ge 2 ] && [ ${#args[@]} -le 5 ] || { usage >&2; exit 2; }

name=${args[0]}
iso=${args[1]}
[ ${#args[@]} -ge 3 ] && vcpus=${args[2]}
[ ${#args[@]} -ge 4 ] && memory=${args[3]}
[ ${#args[@]} -ge 5 ] && maxmem=${args[4]}
[ -n "$maxmem" ] || maxmem=$memory

# --- checks (nothing is changed until they all pass) --------------------

command -v qvm-create >/dev/null 2>&1 ||
    die "qvm-create not found: run this in dom0"

[[ $name =~ ^[a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9_.-]{0,30}$ ]] ||
    die "invalid qube name '$name' (letter first; letters, digits, _ . -; max 31)"
qvm-check -q "$name" 2>/dev/null &&
    die "a qube named '$name' already exists"

case $iso in
    *:/*) iso_vm=${iso%%:*}; iso_path=${iso#*:};;
    *)    die "ISO must be ISO_VM:/absolute/path, e.g. untrusted:/home/user/guix.iso";;
esac
[ -n "$iso_vm" ] || die "ISO must be ISO_VM:/absolute/path"
case $iso_path in *.iso) ;; *) echo "$prog: warning: '$iso_path' doesn't end in .iso" >&2;; esac
# Which installer: the qubes-guix ISO runs the qrexec agent, so dom0 must
# wait for it (the qrexec feature); a stock installer would be killed at
# qrexec_timeout for not answering.
if [ "$qubes_iso" = auto ]; then
    case ${iso_path##*/} in
        *qubes-guix*) qubes_iso=1;;
        *) if [ "$install" = 1 ]; then qubes_iso=1; else qubes_iso=0; fi;;
    esac
fi
if [ "$install" = 1 ] && [ "$qubes_iso" = 0 ]; then
    die "--unattended with an ISO needs the qubes-guix installer ISO (iso/qubes-guix-iso).
With the stock Guix ISO: create the qube, do the GRUB step, then run
  $prog --unattended $name"
fi

is_uint "$vcpus"  && [ "$vcpus" -ge 1 ]     || die "VCPUS must be a positive integer"
is_uint "$memory" && [ "$memory" -ge 1024 ] || die "MEMORY must be at least 1024 (MiB)"
is_uint "$maxmem"                           || die "MAXMEM must be an integer (MiB)"
[ "$maxmem" -ge "$memory" ] || die "MAXMEM ($maxmem) is less than MEMORY ($memory)"
[ "$memory" -ge 3000 ] ||
    echo "$prog: warning: MEMORY $memory MiB is tight for 'guix system init'; 4000 is safer" >&2

if [ -n "$netvm" ]; then
    qvm-check -q "$netvm" 2>/dev/null || die "network qube '$netvm' doesn't exist"
fi

if [ "$iso_vm" = dom0 ]; then
    [ -f "$iso_path" ] || die "no such file in dom0: $iso_path"
else
    qvm-check -q "$iso_vm" 2>/dev/null || die "qube '$iso_vm' doesn't exist"
    # The ISO is attached from a running qube, so start it now (also needed
    # to look for the file).
    if ! qvm-check -q --running "$iso_vm" 2>/dev/null; then
        run qvm-start "$iso_vm"
    fi
    if [ "$dry_run" = 0 ] || qvm-check -q --running "$iso_vm" 2>/dev/null; then
        qvm-run -q --pass-io --no-gui --no-shell "$iso_vm" test -f "$iso_path" </dev/null ||
            die "no such file in $iso_vm: $iso_path"
    fi
fi

# --unattended: the password now, not after the boot.
[ "$install" = 1 ] && ask_password

# --- create -------------------------------------------------------------

created=0
cleanup() {
    status=$?
    if [ $status -ne 0 ] && [ "$created" = 1 ] && [ "$dry_run" = 0 ]; then
        echo "$prog: failed; removing the half-made qube '$name'" >&2
        qvm-kill "$name" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
        qvm-remove -f "$name" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
    fi
}
trap cleanup EXIT

if [ "$template" = 1 ]; then
    class=TemplateVM
    # Templates get no netvm by default, but the installer and the first
    # guix pull need one. Afterwards it can go (updates via the proxy).
    [ -n "$netvm" ] || netvm=$(qubes-prefs default_netvm)
    [ -n "$netvm" ] || die "no default netvm; give one with --netvm"
else
    class=StandaloneVM
fi

echo "Creating $name: $class, HVM, $vcpus vCPU, $memory-$maxmem MiB, root $root_size"

run qvm-create --class "$class" --label "$label" \
    --property virt_mode=hvm "$name"
created=1

# The kernel comes from the qube's own disk (GRUB), not from dom0.
run qvm-prefs "$name" kernel ''
run qvm-prefs "$name" vcpus "$vcpus"
run qvm-prefs "$name" memory "$memory"
run qvm-prefs "$name" maxmem "$maxmem"
# With memory balancing on, a Guix boot can take over a minute to reach the
# qrexec agent (83 s measured), past the 60 s default; on a timeout,
# qvm-start kills the domain uncleanly. Without balancing it's ~25 s.
run qvm-prefs "$name" qrexec_timeout 300
[ -n "$netvm" ] && run qvm-prefs "$name" netvm "$netvm"
run qvm-volume extend "$name:root" "$root_size"

# The Qubes Update tool can't update Guix (guix pull + reconfigure does).
run qvm-features "$name" skip-update 1

# A template's updates go through the Qubes updates proxy (qubes.UpdatesProxy),
# so it can do without a netvm once installed. The guest reads this at boot.
[ "$template" = 1 ] && run qvm-service "$name" updates-proxy-setup on

# Memory balancing is maxmem alone (set above; 0 here: off). It only takes
# effect once the agent runs meminfo-writer; until then the qube simply has
# MEMORY. Not the meminfo-writer service: Qubes derives it from maxmem, and
# setting it resets maxmem to 0.
[ "$maxmem" -gt "$memory" ] || run qvm-prefs "$name" maxmem 0

clients_setup "$name"

if [ "$qubes_iso" = 1 ]; then
    # qvm-start then waits for the installer's qrexec agent (up to
    # qrexec_timeout), so everything after it can use qvm-run.
    run qvm-features "$name" qrexec 1
    echo "Booting the qubes-guix installer; waiting for its qrexec agent."
fi
run qvm-start "$name" --cdrom="$iso_vm:$iso_path"
created=0           # it exists and boots: keep it from here on

if [ "$install" = 1 ]; then
    t0=$(date +%s)
    unattended
    exit 0
fi

if [ "$qubes_iso" = 1 ]; then
    [ "$dry_run" = 1 ] && { echo; echo "(dry run: nothing was changed)"; exit 0; }
    opts="$( [ "$template" = 1 ] && echo ' --template')$( [ "$branch_given" = 1 ] && echo " -b $branch")"
    cat <<EOF

$name is running the qubes-guix installer, with its network and qrexec
agent up. Either:

- unattended, in dom0:
    $prog --unattended$opts $name
  It asks for a password, partitions, installs and powers off, then starts
  $name from its disk and updates it.

- or with the installer's dialogs, in $name's console window. Its network
  step may find no device to configure: continue, the network is already
  up. At 'Installation complete', DON'T reboot; in dom0:
    $prog --finish-install$opts $name
  It makes the new system a qube, then restarts $name from its disk.

A root shell: the console's VT3, or from dom0
  qvm-run -u root --pass-io $name 'COMMAND'
EOF
    exit 0
fi

# --- what the installer needs -------------------------------------------

pref() { qvm-prefs "$name" "$1" 2>/dev/null || true; }

if [ "$dry_run" = 1 ]; then
    echo
    echo "(dry run: nothing was changed)"
    exit 0
fi

ip=$(pref ip)
gw=$(pref visible_gateway)
dns=$(pref dns | xargs)
[ -n "$dns" ] || dns="10.139.1.1 10.139.1.2"

# The installer's console is the emulated VGA window. dom0's desktop grabs
# Alt+Fn, so console switches go through xdotool (click the window when the
# cursor turns into a cross).
vt_key() { echo "     xdotool key --window \$(xdotool selectwindow) $1"; }

cat <<EOF

$name is booting the Guix installer in its console window. To install:

1. At the GRUB menu, with 'GNU Guix installation' highlighted, press 'e'.
   Go to the end of the 'linux' line (it ends 'modprobe.blacklist=radeon,
   amdgpu'), add  nomodeset  and boot it (Ctrl+X). Without it the console
   on VT3 doesn't work. The installed system doesn't need it.
EOF

if [ -z "$ip" ]; then
    cat <<EOF

2. $name has no network qube, so the installer will be offline. Give it
   one (qvm-prefs $name netvm <qube>) and restart it.
EOF
else
    cat <<EOF

2. Qubes has no DHCP, so the network is set by hand. Easiest, in dom0:
     $prog --type-network $name
   (click the console window when asked; it switches to the shell on VT3
   and types the commands). Or type them yourself after Ctrl+Alt+F3
   (check the interface name with 'ip -br link'):
     ip addr add $ip/8 dev eth0
     ip link set eth0 up
     ip route add default via $gw
EOF
    for d in $dns; do echo "     echo nameserver $d >> /etc/resolv.conf"; done
    cat <<EOF
   The /8 puts the gateway on-link; with /32 the default route is refused.
   Keep these for the network section of your first config.scm:
     address $ip/8, gateway $gw, DNS $dns
EOF
fi

cat <<EOF

3. Switch to the installer screen. In dom0:
$(vt_key ctrl+alt+F1)

4. Unattended: at the installer's first screen, in dom0:
     $prog --unattended$( [ "$template" = 1 ] && echo ' --template')$( [ "$branch" != main ] && echo " -b $branch") $name
   (skip steps 2 and 3: it sets the network itself). It asks for a password,
   then types the rest: partition, config.scm (Xfce, user 'user'), the
   qubes channel, guix system init, power off; then starts $name again.

   Or install as usual, up to the 'Installation complete' screen. DON'T
   reboot yet. In dom0:
     $prog --finish-install$( [ "$template" = 1 ] && echo ' --template')$( [ "$branch" != main ] && echo " -b $branch") --run $name
   (click the console window when asked). On VT3 it clones the channel
   and makes the installed system a qube: config.scm wrapped in
   (qubes-operating-system ...), then 'guix system init' with the qubes
   channel. Without --run it only shows the command, for you to review
   and type. When VT3 says Done, reboot. That's the last manual step:
   the first boot has its network, qrexec, the GUI agent, passwordless
   sudo for the qubes group, and the qubes channel for guix pull.

   An already-installed and rebooted system: log in as root on VT3, then
     $prog --bootstrap$( [ "$template" = 1 ] && echo ' --template') $name
   types /tmp/qubes-guix-bootstrap.sh there (network, clone, setup); read
   it, then 'sh /tmp/qubes-guix-bootstrap.sh'.

   If the console window goes away once the Qubes GUI agent runs, get it
   back with:  qvm-start-daemon --force-stubdomain $name
EOF
